      "page_title": "Classic Bugs & Choosing a Tool",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Four Classic Bugs"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mistake",
            "Damage"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "return True in __exit__",
              "eats every exception"
            ],
            [
              "bare yield, no finally",
              "teardown skipped on raise"
            ],
            [
              "reuse one @contextmanager",
              "RuntimeError on 2nd entry"
            ],
            [
              "acquire in __init__",
              "leaks if never entered"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Partial Setup Leaks",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "__enter__ that raises after acquiring #1 leaks it",
            "__exit__ never runs, so nothing releases #1",
            "Fix: hold an internal ExitStack while setting up"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def __enter__(self):",
            "    with ExitStack() as s:",
            "        self.f = s.enter_context(open(p))",
            "        self.c = s.enter_context(connect())",
            "        self._stack = s.pop_all()   # safe"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Stale Name After Block",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "with open(p) as f:",
            "    data = f.read()",
            "f.read()  # ValueError: I/O",
            "          # operation on closed file"
          ],
          "note": "name survives, file is closed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Scope Discipline"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`suppress(Exception)` over a big block hides real failures",
            "It also skips the rest of the block silently",
            "`with` never swallows errors — it guarantees cleanup only",
            "Holding a lock for a whole function hurts concurrency",
            "Deep nesting is right-drift with no added safety"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Choosing an Approach",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Tool",
            "Best for",
            "Catch"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "try/finally",
              "one-off cleanup",
              "repeated everywhere"
            ],
            [
              "class",
              "reuse, state",
              "more code"
            ],
            [
              "@contextmanager",
              "linear pair",
              "single-use"
            ],
            [
              "ExitStack",
              "dynamic count",
              "explicit calls"
            ],
            [
              "suppress",
              "discarding only",
              "very narrow"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The Contract",
          "text": "__enter__ raises: no __exit__. Otherwise __exit__ always runs. A truthy __exit__ return suppresses. Cleanup order is LIFO in every mechanism."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
